  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 PM EST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nNow - Thursday Night...\n\nThe surface cold front is now pushing into the I-65 corridor, and \nwill continue its slow push eastward to the I-75 corridor by early \nevening. Ahead of this feature, the 30-40 knot low level jet core \nwill continue to lift to the NE, with periodic rain showers \ncontinuing across the eastern half of our CWA into the evening. \nStill watching a cluster of stronger showers and a few storms across \nour SE CWA where the nose of a meager instability axis resides. A \nfew stronger storms with locally gusty wind remains a threat down \nthere for the next 1 to 2 hours. \n\nOutside of that, looks like rainfall totals are mostly ending up in \nthe .75\" to 1.5\" range, with some clusters of 2.25\" - 2.5\" down \nacross south central KY. Overall, a pretty beneficial rainfall \nespecially for areas that had crept into D1-D2 drought conditions. \n\nThings dry out from W - E heading into tonight, with some low level \nstratocu building in post-frontal. Time heights suggest that enough \nlow level moisture may reside for some lingering patchy drizzle \nthrough around Midnight EST or so. This will be interesting as SDF \nhas currently tied its daily record rainfall for November 4th at \n1.47\". Any drizzle could put us over the record. LEX has already \nbroken its daily rainfall record with plenty of precipitation still \nto come. \n\nShould dry out after Midnight EST with skies becoming mostly clear \nassuming the drying isn't overdone and post-frontal stratocu hangs \nin longer than expected. Temps should stay up a bit given light to \nsteady winds behind the front. Looking for upper 40s and low 50s. \nThursday should be pleasant with perhaps some upper sky cover and \ntemperatures reaching the mid 60s. Cooler night Thursday night with \nlows mostly in the low 40s.\n\nFriday - Wednesday...\n\nA closed upper low will slide into the eastern Great Lakes region on \nFriday, with a trough axis expected to slide through our region as \nwell. Looks like some low level moisture will pool in the 1000-800 \nmb range associated with these features, and could be enough to \nsqueeze out some very light right rain and/or patchy drizzle. \nOtherwise, the extended portion of the forecast looks dry with \nunremarkable NW flow aloft and surface high pressure in control. We \nwill have to watch for a cutoff low passing to our south around the \nTuesday time frame, but for the moment the data suggests a dry \nforecast.\n\nFriday and Saturday will be the coolest of this forecast cycle with \nhighs in the mid 50s to around 60. Looks like some frosty mornings \nstarting Saturday morning through Wednesday morning, however Sunday \nand Monday mornings may carry some freeze potential as well. In \ntalking with UK Ag, this could be the cold spell that ends our \nfrost/freeze headlines for the year given that the horticulture \nseason is all but over. High temperatures do trend a bit milder back \ninto the 60s for early to middle of next week.",
